    "title": "NASA Astronauts Don’t Receive Overtime Pay for Space Mission But Get $5 a Day",
    "text": "AdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENTOvertime Pay for 9 Extra Months in Space? Nope.Suni Williams and Butch Wilmore do not get overtime for their unexpectedly long stay on the International Space Station, according to NASA rules. But they do get $5 a day for “incidentals.”Butch Wilmore, a NASA astronaut, is helped out of a SpaceX Dragon craft after it landed off the Gulf Coast of Florida on Tuesday.Credit...NASA, via ReutersMarch 20, 2025If your eight-day work trip was unexpectedly extended by nine months, you might expect to rack up some overtime pay.Not so for Suni Williams and Butch Wilmore, the NASA astronauts who spent 278 extra days on the International Space Station after their spacecraft malfunctioned. On Tuesday, they splashed down off the Gulf Coast of Florida, ending a saga that had captivated the country since last summer.But despite their far-flung destination, and the danger and romance of space travel, when it comes to pay, Ms. Williams and Mr. Wilmore are treated effectively like any other government employee who takes a business trip to the next state over.Subscribe to The Times to read as many articles as you like.Yan Zhuang is a Times reporter in Seoul who covers breaking news.A version of this article appears in print on March 22, 2025, Section A, Page 1 of the New York edition with the headline: 278 Unexpected Days on the Space Station Pays What, Now?. Order Reprints | Today’s Paper | SubscribeRelated ContentAdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENT",
